    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The stronger legal position for a moot court on this topic is that the expansion of executive privilege is a dangerous erosion of the separation of powers and a threat to democratic accountability. While the Framers intended the President to have some discretion to communicate freely with foreign sovereigns and internal counsel, modern absolute claims often unjustifiably shield illegal activities, cover-up attempts, or justice with the convenient excuse of \"impending discovery.\" Relying on unchecked privilege destroys the essential \"humming box\" required to keep the executive branch honest and prevents the legislature and judiciary from effectively conducting their due processes.",
